WEBVTT

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:07.451
In October 1972, Cybersyn's telex lines ran from the north of Chile to the south.

00:00:09.151 --> 00:00:14.384
Every afternoon, each factory sent its numbers along them, one way, to Santiago.

00:00:15.884 --> 00:00:23.623
That month, truck owners went on strike against a plan for a state trucking company, and shopkeepers, bank clerks and others joined them.

00:00:25.123 --> 00:00:31.229
With most trucks standing still, shops and factories across the country could run out of essential goods.

00:00:32.929 --> 00:00:36.182
A report once a day, one way, couldn't keep goods moving.

00:00:37.282 --> 00:00:42.452
The government needed to hear, as it happened, where goods were short and which trucks could still run.

00:00:44.152 --> 00:00:51.425
So Fernando Flores, who had started Cybersyn at CORFO, set up an emergency operations centre in Santiago.

00:00:52.925 --> 00:01:00.201
There, the Cybersyn team and officials read about two thousand telexes a day, from all over the country, and answered them.

00:01:01.701 --> 00:01:06.688
The same lines now carried new messages, many times a day, in both directions.

00:01:08.388 --> 00:01:15.686
With them, about two hundred trucks still running were sent where they were needed most, against the effects of forty thousand on strike.

00:01:17.386 --> 00:01:22.800
At the utility, Inés lists every path that crosses the wall between the network and retail.

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:26.782
She finds eleven. Only four are on the map.

00:01:34.282 --> 00:01:41.290
One is a link into the meter system's database, made years ago so the complaints team could check a reading during a call.

00:01:43.190 --> 00:01:52.298
It shows every connection point's readings: all 540,000, the 230,000 served by other retailers among them.

00:01:54.198 --> 00:01:59.722
That's what the wall is there to stop: customer information stays with the business that collected it.

00:02:01.622 --> 00:02:08.338
A path that isn't on the map has no owner at either end. When one end changes, the other finds out when it breaks.

00:02:10.038 --> 00:02:18.137
In March, one such path, a nightly script, wrote for months to a queue nothing read. About nineteen hundred bills went uncorrected.

00:02:21.637 --> 00:02:29.587
In November, the Mendozas' meter, at 14 Quarry Lane, stopped sending readings for nine days, while a radio mast was mended.

00:02:31.287 --> 00:02:34.999
The network estimated those days, and billing used the estimate.

00:02:36.299 --> 00:02:41.168
When the meter came back, it sent what it had stored, and the network replaced the estimate.

00:02:42.868 --> 00:02:47.485
Now the replacement has to cross the wall, and the agreed list says what may cross.

00:02:48.985 --> 00:02:51.579
For a replaced reading, it lists five things.

00:02:52.479 --> 00:02:59.745
The connection point, by its network ID; the days; the estimate it replaces; the readings; and when it was replaced.

00:03:01.845 --> 00:03:08.505
Each retailer gets them only for the connection points it serves, the group's own retailer no differently from the rest.

00:03:10.205 --> 00:03:12.066
Nothing else from the meter crosses.

00:03:15.566 --> 00:03:19.046
How it travels is a choice too, and DAMA names three shapes.

00:03:20.546 --> 00:03:25.524
Point to point: one system wired straight to another, like the complaints team's link.

00:03:26.824 --> 00:03:33.077
It's quick to build. But each new reader adds a wire, and a change at one end can break the other, unseen.

00:03:34.977 --> 00:03:42.001
Hub and spoke: every system sends to one hub, and reads from it, as Cybersyn's factories sent to Santiago.

00:03:43.901 --> 00:03:51.117
Publish and subscribe: the home sends each change once, as it happens, and each reader allowed to read it subscribes.

00:03:53.017 --> 00:03:58.643
Which one fits depends on three questions: who reads it, when they act on it, and what each may see.

00:04:02.343 --> 00:04:08.373
The replaced reading has many readers: every retailer, and in each, billing and customer service.

00:04:09.873 --> 00:04:15.630
Billing acts on it at the next bill. Customer service acts on it while a customer is on the phone.

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:20.322
And each retailer may see only its own customers'.

00:04:21.622 --> 00:04:28.599
So the network publishes each replaced reading once, when it's replaced, and each retailer receives only its own.

00:04:30.699 --> 00:04:33.964
Within the hour is soon enough: nobody acts on it sooner.

00:04:35.264 --> 00:04:40.985
The complaints team's link is closed: they read the same messages as the rest of customer service.

00:04:44.685 --> 00:04:51.596
The flow gets a contract, agreed by the two teams at its ends: Grace's, which sends it, and billing's, which reads it.

00:04:53.296 --> 00:04:57.027
It keeps to the agreed list, so they need nobody else's sign-off.

00:04:58.527 --> 00:05:01.359
It says what crosses, and what each part means.

00:05:02.659 --> 00:05:05.270
How soon: within an hour of the replacement.

00:05:06.570 --> 00:05:13.550
What happens when one is late or wrong: the network's team is told that day, and corrects it at home, never in the copy.

00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:22.898
And how it changes: parts may be added, but none removed or renamed without a new version, and readers are told first.

00:05:26.998 --> 00:05:34.703
On the data practice on the map, the flow is drawn across the wall: from the meter reading in meter data, to its copy in billing.

00:05:36.603 --> 00:05:42.001
It names its shape and its contract, and an owner at each end: Grace, and the head of retail.

00:05:43.701 --> 00:05:46.704
Every path across the wall goes on the map, or goes.

00:05:48.004 --> 00:05:53.606
Five become flows, each with its contract. The other six, the link among them, are closed.

00:05:55.306 --> 00:06:00.958
How each flow is built, with connectors, events and pipelines, is the series Data that moves.

00:06:06.858 --> 00:06:10.992
In October 1972, Chile's telex lines didn't change.

00:06:12.092 --> 00:06:17.986
What crossed them did: new messages, many times a day, both ways, in time for whoever could act.

00:06:19.686 --> 00:06:26.653
At the utility, every fact that crosses the wall now crosses on purpose: drawn, with its shape and its contract.

00:06:28.153 --> 00:06:32.746
On the twenty-ninth of November, the Mendozas' meter sent its nine stored days.

00:06:34.046 --> 00:06:38.246
The estimate was replaced at 10:14, and billing had it a minute later.

00:06:39.746 --> 00:06:44.079
Their December bill charged for what they really used, and nobody had to call.

00:06:45.779 --> 00:06:49.468
That month, 2,140 estimates were replaced.

00:06:50.568 --> 00:06:56.275
All but nine reached their retailer within the hour, and those nine were found and sent the same day.

00:06:57.775 --> 00:07:04.350
Each domain now sends the others what they need. But who owns which data, and who answers for what's made from it?
